@.str.263 = private unnamed_addr constant [1127 x i8] c"\0A\0A# Examples\0A\0A* Apply the build environment of GNU hello to the current shell:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # . <(nix print-dev-env nixpkgs#hello)\0A  ```\0A\0A* Get the build environment in JSON format:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix print-dev-env nixpkgs#hello --json\0A  ```\0A\0A  The output will look like this:\0A\0A  ```json\0A  {\0A    \22bashFunctions\22: {\0A      \22buildPhase\22: \22 \\n    runHook preBuild;\\n...\22,\0A      ...\0A    },\0A    \22variables\22: {\0A      \22src\22: {\0A        \22type\22: \22exported\22,\0A        \22value\22: \22/nix/store/3x7dwzq014bblazs7kq20p9hyzz0qh8g-hello-2.10.tar.gz\22\0A      },\0A      \22postUnpackHooks\22: {\0A        \22type\22: \22array\22,\0A        \22value\22: [\22_updateSourceDateEpochFromSourceRoot\22]\0A      },\0A      ...\0A    }\0A  }\0A  ```\0A\0A# Description\0A\0AThis command prints a shell script that can be sourced by `bash` and\0Athat sets the variables and shell functions defined by the build\0Aprocess of [*installable*](./nix.md#installables). This allows you to get a similar build\0Aenvironment in your current shell rather than in a subshell (as with\0A`nix develop`).\0A\0AWith `--json`, the output is a JSON serialisation of the variables and\0Afunctions defined by the build process.\0A\0A\00", align 1
